Thank goodness for the travel command.

Around 11:00pm EST in the 2005 tournament I decided to try to finish
the Archaelogist I had started the night before that was before the
Castle but just after the quest.  Time went by, progress was made,
dinner was eaten, and finally the Amulet was mine. Sounds lovely - but
then I noticed the clock showed I had a mere 35 minutes to ascend -
doh!

Naturally, I was lawful, and got thrown back without remorse.  Some
potions came in handy (even wished for more), and I found myself
standing on the upstair with about 20 minutes to spare.  With time
ticking away, I kept my composure, went through my inventory, wrote
some needed scrolls (since i forgot to get my stash on dlvl4), mixed
some potions, and RUSHED through the planes.

When I say rushed, I mean in a don't-try-this-at-home way I smashed
the keyboard's direction and esc buttons alternately furiously with
little regard to HP or enemy.  Potions and amulets were life savers,
and I finally made it to Water.  Looking at the clock I had about 6
minutes to spare. Since the mashing the keyboard isn't great in Water,
I had to search until a sizeable gap appeared which I took to get near
the center.  Once there, i located the portal, and 30 seconds later I
was at the Astral Plane.

I chose to go towards Death first, utilizing some good keyboard
mashing, but the Preists were putting on a hurt...soon my /oTel were
empty, so I just took out the remaining !oFH and charged to the altar.
Naturally it was the wrong one, so I had to retreat with *gasp* 2 min
to go.  My /oDeath took care of Pestillance, but all these Preists and
Angels were in the way.  Mash mash, quaff, mash mash quaff, mash mash,
new amulet, mash mash, check the altar, offer, wohoo!

Looked at my clock...3:02AM EST, with the tourney ending midnight PST.
Doh! I rush over to the tournament website and see the clock shows 1
min, 42 sec remaining...so it appears my clock was a wee bit fast, and
so I beat the buzzer ;)

Highly entertaining. In hindsight, I would have prepared an acid blob
army, but I was a little too frazzled.  Certainly not recommended for
anyone, but it speaks volumes for the satisfaction nethack is a turn
based, and not real time.

